{"name":"Iron Gate","hex":"#585a60","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"iron","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A common, inexpensive metal, silvery grey when untarnished, that rusts, is attracted by magnets, and is used in making steel: a chemical element having atomic number 26 and symbol Fe.","Any material, not a steel, predominantly made of elemental iron."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of the metal iron.","Strong , inflexible."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/iron"},{"term":"gate","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A doorlike structure outside a house.","A doorway, opening, or passage in a fence or wall."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To keep something inside by means of a closed gate.","To punish (a student) by not allowing to leave the college grounds."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gate"}],"entries":[{"title":"Iron Gates (Algeria)","blocks":[{"text":"The Iron Gates are a mountain pass in the Bibans in Algeria. They gave their name to the Biban Range."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Adrien_Dauzats_001.jpg/330px-Adrien_Dauzats_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron_Gates_(Algeria)","wikidata":"Q3062435"},{"title":"Iron Gate (Antioch)","blocks":[{"text":"The Iron Gate is a sixth-century dam which is located in the mountains to the east of Antakya in Turkey. It is one of the earliest known arch dams and is now the oldest to survive. It was built in the mid-sixth century on the order of the Byzantine emperor Justinian I to collect and slow the waters of the Parmenios stream, which in the rainy season could cause torrential floods endangering Antioch's city centre. Previous attempts to channel the floodwaters into underground passageways had not fully solved the problem."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/RUINS_OF_IRON_GATE%2C_ANTIOCH_ANTAKYA%2C_TURKEY_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-RUINS_OF_IRON_GATE%2C_ANTIOCH_ANTAKYA%2C_TURKEY_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron_Gate_(Antioch)","wikidata":"Q139554287"},{"title":"Iron","blocks":[{"text":"Iron is a chemical element; it has symbol Fe and atomic number 26. It is a metal that belongs to the first transition series and group 8 of the periodic table. It is, by mass, the most common element on Earth, forming much of Earth's outer and inner core. It is the fourth most abundant element in the Earth's crust. In its metallic state it was mainly deposited by meteorites."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Iron_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg/330px-Iron_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron","wikidata":"Q677"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Adrien_Dauzats_001.jpg/500px-Adrien_Dauzats_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Adrien_Dauzats_001.jpg/500px-Adrien_Dauzats_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"The Iron Gates Pass in Algeria, 18 October 1839\", by Adrien Dauzats.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAdrien_Dauzats_001.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Bibanar.jpg/500px-Bibanar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Bibanar.jpg/1280px-Bibanar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Bibanar.jpg/500px-Bibanar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thu